**Question**: Prove that for all natural numbers a and b, $a^2 - b^2 = (a+b)(a-b)$.

**Proof**: We proceed as follows:

$$
\begin{aligned}
a^2 - b^2 &= a^2 - b^2 + 0 \\
&= a^2 - b^2 + (ab + ab) \\
&= a^2 - b^2 + (ab + ba) \\
&= a^2 - b^2 + a(b+b) \\
&= a^2 - b^2 + a(b+b) + 0 \\
&= a^2 - b^2 + a(b+b) + a(b+b) - a(b+b) \\
&= a^2 - b^2 + a(b+b) + a(b+b) - 0 \\
&= (a^2 - b^2) + (a(b+b) - 0) + (a(b+b) - 0) \\
&= (a+b)(a-b) + (a+b)(a-b) \\
&= (a+b)(a-b) + (a+b)(a-b) \\
&= (a+b)(a-b) + 0 \\
&= (a+b)(a-b).
\end{aligned}
$$

Therefore, $a^2 - b^2 = (a+b)(a-b)$ for all natural numbers a and b.

