We use induction on $p$ to prove this statement.

Base case: If $p = 0$, then $n \times p = 0$, and so $x - n \times p = x$. Therefore, $\frac{x - n \times p}{n} = \frac{x}{n}$, and $\frac{x}{n} - p = \frac{x}{n} - 0 = \frac{x}{n}$. Thus, the statement holds for the base case.

Inductive step: Assume that the statement holds for some $p$. That is, assume that $\frac{x - n \times p}{n} = \frac{x}{n} - p$. We want to show that the statement also holds for $p + 1$. That is, we want to show that $\frac{x - n \times (p + 1)}{n} = \frac{x}{n} - (p + 1)$.

We have

$$\begin{align*}
\frac{x - n \times (p + 1)}{n} &= \frac{x - (n \times p + n)}{n} \\
&= \frac{x - n \times p - n}{n} \\
&= \frac{x - n \times p}{n} - \frac{n}{n} \\
&= \frac{x - n \times p}{n} - 1.
\end{align*}$$

By the induction hypothesis, we have $\frac{x - n \times p}{n} = \frac{x}{n} - p$. Therefore,

$$\begin{align*}
\frac{x - n \times (p + 1)}{n} &= \frac{x - n \times p}{n} - 1 \\
&= \frac{x}{n} - p - 1 \\
&= \frac{x}{n} - (p + 1).
\end{align*}$$

Thus, the statement holds for $p + 1$.

Therefore, by induction, the statement holds for all natural numbers $p$.

