If $i$ is not a maximal element in a well-founded partial order, then $i$ is equal to the iterate of the predecessor function $n$ times followed by the iterate of the successor function $n$ times.

Let $i \in A$, where $A$ is a well-founded partial order.  Assume $i$ is not a maximal element in $A$.  This means there exists $j \in A$ such that $i \prec j$.  We will prove that
$$
\operatorname{pred}^n (\operatorname{succ}^n (i)) = i
$$
by induction on $n$.  The base case is $n = 0$.  In this case, the left-hand side of the equation is $\operatorname{pred}^0 (\operatorname{succ}^0 (i)) = \operatorname{id} (i) = i$, which is equal to the right-hand side.

For the inductive step, assume the equation holds for some $n \in \mathbb{N}$.  We will show that it also holds for $n+1$.  The left-hand side of the equation for $n+1$ is
$$
\operatorname{pred}^{n+1} (\operatorname{succ}^{n+1} (i)) = \operatorname{pred} (\operatorname{pred}^n (\operatorname{succ}^n (\operatorname{succ} (i))))
$$
By the inductive hypothesis, $\operatorname{pred}^n (\operatorname{succ}^n (i)) = i$.  So the left-hand side of the equation for $n+1$ is
$$
\operatorname{pred} (\operatorname{pred}^n (\operatorname{succ}^n (\operatorname{succ} (i)))) = \operatorname{pred} (i)
$$
By the definition of the predecessor function, $\operatorname{pred} (i) = j$.  So the left-hand side of the equation for $n+1$ is $j$.  The right-hand side of the equation for $n+1$ is $i$.  So the equation holds for $n+1$.

Therefore, by the principle of mathematical induction, the equation
$$
\operatorname{pred}^n (\operatorname{succ}^n (i)) = i
$$
holds for all $n \in \mathbb{N}$.

