You are provided with a theorem and its informal statement and proof:

Let \(G\) be a group. If \(a \times x = y\), prove that for any integer \(m\), \(a \times x^m = y^m\).

We proceed by induction on \(m\). 

For \(m=0\), \(x^m = 1\), so \(a \times x^0 = a = y^0\). Now suppose for some \(n \in \mathbb{N}\), \(a \times x^n = y^n\). Then by the semiconjugacy definition, \(x = a^{-1} y a\). Thus
$$
\begin{split}
a \times x^{n+1} &= a \times x^n \times x  \\\
&= y^n \times x \\\
&= y^n \times a^{-1} y a   \\\
&= (y^n \times a^{-1}) \times (y \times a)   \\\
&= y^{n+1}
\end{split}
$$

Hence \(a \times x^{n+1} = y^{n+1}\). By the Principle of Mathematical Induction, it follows that for any integer \(m\), \(a \times x^m = y^m\).

