Suppose \(|x-y| = |x-z|\), then \(|x-y|^2=|x-z|^2\). Expanding the squares produces \((x-y)^2=(x-z)^2 \implies (x-y)(x-y)=(x-z)(x-z) \). Factoring the left hand side gives \((x-y)^2=(x-y)^2\), and subtracting \((x-y)^2\) produces 
$$0=(x-z)(x-z)-(x-y)(x-y) =(x-z)(x-z)-(x-z+z-y)(x-z) $$ 

$$0=(x-z)(x-z)-(x^2-xz +xz -zy+zx-z^2)$$
$$0=-z^2+x^2+2xz-xz$$
$$0=-z^2+x^2+xz$$ 
$$z^2=x^2+xz$$
$$\frac{z^2}{x}=x+z \implies  x\frac{z^2}{x}=x^2+xz \implies  z^2=x^2+xz$$ 
$$z^2-x^2=xz$$
$$(z+x)(z-x)=xz \implies z+x = \frac{xz}{z-x} $$

Since the equation holds for all real numbers, we can substitute \(x\) with \(y\): 
$$x+y = \frac{xz}{z-x} = \frac{yz}{z-y}.$$

Multiplying the equation by \((z-x) \) and \((z-y) \) gives \(z-y=z-x \implies y=x\)

Conversely, if \(y=z \), then 
$$|x-y|= |x-z| \iff |x-z|=|x-z|$$

