Show that the value projection is injective. That is, if \(x^i = y^i\), then \(x=y\).

Let \(x^i = y^i\). Then, \(x^i \cdot 1 = y^i \cdot 1\). By associativity of multiplication, \(x^i \cdot 1 = x \cdot x^{i-1}\) and \(y^i \cdot 1 = y \cdot y^{i-1}\). By the definition of exponentiation, \(x^{i-1} = 1\) and \(y^{i-1} = 1\). Substituting these into the previous equations, we get \(x = y^i\) and \(y = x^i\). By symmetry, \(x = y\).

Therefore, the value projection is injective.

